<TopicPage slug="subjects/design/intermediate/grids-and-layout">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Layout is <strong>arrangement as argument</strong>: grids supply the order, whitespace supplies
    the relationships, and the reader\'s eye follows both without ever knowing they were there —
    the design lesson\'s hierarchy, spatially engineered.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="scaffolding, columns, whitespace, responsive" widget="equationStep" config={gridCfg} />

  <h2>Layout patterns that work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>F-pattern and Z-pattern:</strong> eye-tracking (the perception lesson\'s saccades) shows readers scan predictably — put the important things where the scan goes; then break the pattern deliberately when you need attention.</li>
    <li><strong>Modular scales:</strong> sizes from a ratio (1.25×: 12, 15, 19, 24…) rather than arbitrary values — the music lesson\'s harmony (ratios) applied to sizes; coherence for free.</li>
    <li><strong>Asymmetry beats centred everything:</strong> the centred layout is the default of no decision; asymmetric balance (a heavy element balanced by white space and a small counterweight) is where composition begins (the visual-arts lesson\'s composition, page-shaped).</li>
    <li><strong>Card layouts, honestly assessed:</strong> the design lesson\'s critique of the SaaS-card default — grids of identical cards are coherent and often lazy; ask what arrangement THIS content argues for (editorial? dashboard? narrative?).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Müller-Brockmann, Grid Systems; Lupton, Graphic Design: The New Basics; the perception lesson.</p>
</TopicPage>
